Jym <moyen@loria.fr> writes:
> How are bones files named ? (on Linux)
>
> I currently have the following bones files :
> jym@xrousse:~/slashem$ ls bon*
> bonD0.5.gz  bonD0.7.gz  bonD0.O.gz  bonD0.T.gz  bonM0.3.gz
> 
> I guess that D0.n are dungeon level n and M0.n is mines level n.
> But that seems strange with D0.O and D0.T. Maybe special dungeon levels (O
> would be Oracle and T ? Maybe the Mall ?)
> 
> So, how are bones files named ?

Judging from the directory name, you're playing SLASH'EM; you might
want to tag your subject line to show that, since it does make a
difference (SLASH'EM having many more special levels than vanilla).

The schema used on your platform is
  bon<branchid><roleid>.<levelid>.<compression-suffix>

where:

branchid is a single letter, one of:

D Dungeons of Doom
G Gehennom
M Gnomish Mines
Q Quest
T Vlad's Tower
(there are other letters defined, but those are for branches that can't
leave bones, so are unused)

roleid is the three-letter code for your role in the case of the Quest,
or 0 otherwise

levelid is either the level number within the branch for normal levels of
that branch, or a single letter for specials, one of:

T Mall
O Oracle
R Rats (this conflicts with the Rogue level, which is defined out by
  default; I haven't tried what would happen if you turn REINCARNATION
  on, but I suspect unexpected behaviour)
K Kobold
N Nymph
B Big Room
S Storage room
G Guild of Disgruntled Adventurers

V Valley of the Dead
J Juiblex
E Yeenoghu
O Orcus
D Demogorgon
X middle level of the Wizard's Tower
B Baalzebub
R Geryon
S Dispater
A Asmodeus
G Fake wizard's tower without the portal

T Minetown
E Mine's End

L Quest Locate level

(ditto for the existence of unused letters.)

And compression-suffix, in your case, denotes gzip.

(I think these are all accurate, but I may have missed some oddities
in the way SLASH'EM does bones eligibility.)
  
